#
# The mono led0 heartbeat's own symbols, rsourced by apps/dongle,
# apps/dongle_thread and apps/dongle_ti - every application that ends its boot
# sequence by handing the main thread to the blink loop.
#
# Unlike Kconfig.rgb_led beside it, this one IS sourced by all three. The RGB
# indicator is excluded from apps/dongle_thread because its no-cost-to-the-radio
# argument is about the ws2812 TRANSPORT - a 24-byte SPIM EasyDMA transfer, and
# the ws2812_gpio alternative that would bit-bang with interrupts masked - and
# that argument has to be redone from scratch under MPSL's zero-latency timeslot
# handler. A mono LED has no transport: it is one gpio_pin_set_dt() register
# write from a preemptible thread, with no irq_lock(), no DMA, no shared TIMER
# and no (D)PPI. There is nothing here for the timeslot handler to be exposed
# to, so the exclusion does not transfer.

config ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED
	bool "Flash led0 as a boot/liveness heartbeat"
	depends on GPIO
	default y
	help
	  Compile apps/common/ant_heartbeat_led.c and hand the main thread to it
	  once the transport is up. The LED is found through the `led0`
	  devicetree alias; a board that does not define one gets a main thread
	  that sleeps forever, exactly as before this symbol existed.

	  TURN THIS OFF FOR A BUILD WITH NO LED ACTIVITY AT ALL. With the symbol
	  off the file is not compiled, no GPIO is configured and no pin is ever
	  driven - the board simply boots dark. That is the whole of the
	  off-switch; there is no second knob to find and no overlay to edit.

	  WHAT IT COSTS, since this is the thread that shares a chip with the
	  radio. Two k_sleep() wakeups per cycle - at the defaults below, per
	  4.03 SECONDS - on the main thread, each one a single register write.
	  Nothing here masks interrupts, touches the radio's TIMER or (D)PPI, or
	  runs in interrupt context. The 1 Hz 50 %-duty blink this replaced was
	  eight times as many wakeups.

if ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED

config ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED_ON_MS
	int "Flash length in milliseconds"
	default 30
	range 1 60000
	help
	  30 ms against the 4 s gap below is about 0.74 % duty - a visible blink
	  rather than a lamp, and a bus-powered dongle that is dark 99 % of the
	  time.

	  RAISE THIS, NOT THE DUTY CYCLE, if the flash is too dim to catch on a
	  particular board: shortening _OFF_MS instead would move the anchor the
	  activity curve is scaled against (see _ACTIVITY below) and change what
	  every rate above idle looks like too.

config ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED_OFF_MS
	int "Gap between flashes in milliseconds, when nothing is on the air"
	default 4000
	range 1 60000
	help
	  The gap with _ACTIVITY off, and the IDLE gap with it on - the curve is
	  anchored so that a receive rate of zero produces exactly this value,
	  whatever the curve's own breakpoints say. So the two modes look
	  identical on a quiet bench and cannot drift apart.

config ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED_ACTIVITY
	bool "Shorten the gap as more ANT+ traffic is heard"
	depends on !ANT_DONGLE_ACTIVITY_CLAIMED
	select ANT_DONGLE_ACTIVITY_COUNT
	default y
	help
	  Keeps the flash at _ON_MS and scales the gap from _OFF_MS down towards
	  a floor as sensor traffic rises - roughly 4 s idle, 1.6 s with one
	  sensor, 400 ms with four, 150 ms with ten or so at the default
	  _OFF_MS. It answers "is it hearing anything" on a board whose only
	  output is one LED, which the fixed blink cannot: that only ever
	  answered "did it boot".

	  It reuses the same rate curve as the NeoPixel indicator
	  (apps/common/ant_rgb_led_curve.c, and its ztest suite), so the two
	  read the same air the same way.

	  WHICH IS ALSO WHY IT DEPENDS ON THE NEOPIXEL BEING OFF. They read one
	  counter and ant_activity.h drains it with atomic_clear(), so both at
	  once would each see about half the traffic and both would report a
	  quiet room. A board with a smart LED has the better indicator already;
	  its mono LED falls back to the fixed flash below, which is exactly what
	  the shipping Feather row builds. See ant_activity.h if a board ever
	  genuinely needs both - it needs a second counter, not a second reader.

	  ON BY DEFAULT, AND THAT COMMITS THE HOOK TO EVERY IMAGE. It selects
	  CONFIG_ANT_DONGLE_ACTIVITY_COUNT, so antr_on_message() gains a switch
	  on the message id and one atomic increment per data-bearing message -
	  on the radiant API event thread, never in a radio callback. That is
	  the same code the shipping Feather has run since the NeoPixel landed;
	  what is new is that every board has it. Set this to n to get the flat
	  _ON_MS/_OFF_MS pattern back and the hot path with nothing on it.

	  It costs nothing FURTHER at the LED: the gap is one integer expression
	  evaluated once per flash, on a thread that was going to sleep anyway.

endif # ANT_DONGLE_HEARTBEAT_LED
